<node id="SCF Icon Path Payload">
  <data key="d0">SCF Icon Path Payload</data>
  <data key="d1">PayloadPattern</data>
  <data key="d2">SCF Icon Path Payload is a malicious input construct designed to exploit Server-Side Includes (SSI) vulnerabilities, typically within web applications that improperly process external files referenced by special path indicators. Commonly targeting path traversal and icon.php or other image resource endpoints, this payload exploits misconfigurations in how servers interpret relative or manipulated path parameters. By injecting crafted path strings that reference server-side icons, configuration files, or other sensitive resources within SCF tags, attackers can bypass access controls, execute arbitrary code, or exfiltrate files. Such payloads are often used as part of broader attack scenarios, including Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) or reconnaissance, where the goal is to trick server components into interpreting a path as an icon resource rather than an execution context. This pattern is a reusable technique to probe for and exploit vulnerabilities in servers handling dynamic inclusion of external content through malformed or ambiguous path structures.&lt;SEP&gt;A malformed or specially crafted path injected into Server-Side Include (SSI) processing to trigger unintended behaviors.&lt;SEP&gt;Malicious input designed for Server-Side Components or similar technologies to bypass path restrictions.&lt;SEP&gt;Reusable malicious input crafted to bypass server-side filtering by abusing server configured icon icons or path templates within SSRF detection logic.&lt;SEP&gt;A malicious payload pattern designed to bypass Server-Side Request Forgery or URL path restrictions via specially crafted icon references.&lt;SEP&gt;An input structure targeting Server-Side Component Sourcing (SCF) endpoints with manipulated icon or image path directories.</data>
