<node id="Writable Publish Path">
  <data key="d0">Writable Publish Path</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e60bdd5f7d1090f66c283ab2ebd04906-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-abae2141b512784d1f09780ffa398a5d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d8a71a5db941f76cbd976ed319daa4ce-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5b3691250d8034efc538f45153325e82-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce55c93390355c438aad3e42f2558ea0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e60bdd5f7d1090f66c283ab2ebd04906-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b35a43e83892d75bc8e3746466c2bcfc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8047b6c93b09b89977542720f829a1da-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7eff443bdc3f8249787b9d9ee690d679-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-219fb4a2de2e4f86f2a836460b2e4201-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2af45459b5392b7945360d1fe34d952c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9756198566adacc24b429de0c91a0beb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3a8496df2ccf7a2fedc35b83bd6c5e51-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-198b423c5fcf5fd72465af48d35436e5-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d2">A target condition where the system allows writing to directories or file paths accessible from HTTP or similar protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;Writable Publish Path refers to a directory or file system location on a server that permits write access, allowing data to be uploaded, saved, or modified. This condition is frequently leveraged in security contexts as a vulnerability when such writable locations exist with insufficient access controls. Attackers can exploit a Writable Publish Path to upload malicious content such as scripts or web shells, which can later be accessed or executed to compromise system integrity. The path often features predictable or user-defined naming structures, making it easier for adversaries to identify and target. Writable Publish Paths can arise due to misconfigured file permissions, overly permissive upload mechanisms, or insecure application logic that fails to validate or restrict write operations effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;A target directory or endpoint accessible for file writing or upload functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition where user-uploaded content is written to a globally accessible web directory without sanitization.&lt;SEP&gt;A writable directory or location on the target that accepts uploaded or stored artifacts from the attacker.&lt;SEP&gt;A writable directory or path that allows file uploads or substitutes, useful for webshell or malicious data placement.</data>
  <data key="d1">PreconditionEnvironment</data>
