<node id="Webshell">
  <data key="d0">Webshell</data>
  <data key="d1">Artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A malicious script uploaded to a web server and stored to grant persistent on-demand execution access.&lt;SEP&gt;A malicious script or executable uploaded to a web server to receive commands and interact with the underlying operating system or filesystem.&lt;SEP&gt;A script uploaded to a web server that provides remote Command Execution capabilities after exploitation.&lt;SEP&gt;An uploaded script or executable file that allows persistent server-side access.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-39047a18b515fcf5a6bcfa50dda4ee3a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce55c93390355c438aad3e42f2558ea0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28e61b57c4ef53155963a0bdc0146d46-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7eff443bdc3f8249787b9d9ee690d679-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">htb-cctv-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;htb-devarea-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;htb-guardian-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;htb-monitorsfour-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785756919</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tokens Based Attack">
  <data key="d0">Tokens Based Attack</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-39047a18b515fcf5a6bcfa50dda4ee3a-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d2">A valid authentication session provides context for token forgery or leakage attempts depending on application logic.</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackTechnique</data>
  <data key="d4">htb-cctv-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785755071</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insecure Deserialization">
  <data key="d0">Insecure Deserialization</data>
  <data key="d1">VulnerabilityClass</data>
  <data key="d2">Insecure Deserialization is a weakness class in which an application's deserialization process receives untrusted data and converts it into application-specific object structures without adequate validation or sandboxing. This vulnerability allows adversaries to craft serialized objects containing malicious payloads that, when deserialized, can invoke dangerous methods, execute arbitrary code, or manipulate application data. The core of the weakness lies in the failure to treat serialized input as untrusted, permitting the exploitation of object-oriented features such as method calls, object construction, and the invocation of potentially harmful internal operations. Consequently, successful exploit can lead to unauthorized command execution, data exfiltration, or further compromise of the system.&lt;SEP&gt;A weakness class where untrusted data is deserialized without proper validation, allowing arbitrary object creation or code execution via crafted input.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8a71a5db941f76cbd976ed319daa4ce-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b35a43e83892d75bc8e3746466c2bcfc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b35a43e83892d75bc8e3746466c2bcfc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8047b6c93b09b89977542720f829a1da-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8047b6c93b09b89977542720f829a1da-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6b1a9df875b4f4d10c856b106f068993-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6b1a9df875b4f4d10c856b106f068993-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6ee3143291e5ed56b99323040f30f7d7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6ee3143291e5ed56b99323040f30f7d7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3a8496df2ccf7a2fedc35b83bd6c5e51-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">htb-codetwo-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;htb-gavel-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;htb-giveback-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;htb-hacknet-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;htb-interpreter-methodology.md&lt;SEP&gt;htb-sorcery-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785758010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Credentialed Login Access">
  <data key="d0">Credentialed Login Access</data>
  <data key="d1">AttackerCapability</data>
  <data key="d2">Reusable access using valid credentials issued by the target system, enabling session manipulation and privilege abuse.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d8a71a5db941f76cbd976ed319daa4ce-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">htb-codetwo-methodology.md</data>
  <data key="d5">1785755114</data>
