<edge source="SQL Injection File Read" target="Linux Host">
  <data key="d7">13.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Linux Host, SQL Injection File Read is a cybersecurity attack technique specifically targeted at Linux-based systems or web servers that host relational databases. This approach leverages the Linux operating system environment to exploit vulnerabilities within an application's database interaction logic. The primary objective is to facilitate unauthorized file read operations through malicious SQL injection payloads, often taking advantage of the specific permission model and infrastructure configurations inherent to Linux hosts. As such, the technique is compatible with and observed on Linux-based hosting infrastructures, where it can be executed against applications with insecure database query handling to disclose and extract sensitive files from the local filesystem.&lt;SEP&gt;The target environment operating system influences the effectiveness of filesystem-based attack techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique focuses on exploiting injection vulnerabilities within a service hosted on a Linux-based server.&lt;SEP&gt;The technique often applies to Linux web servers running applications that support SQL and allow dynamic file reading.&lt;SEP&gt;This technique is identified as applicable within a Linux host environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Applies to Linux-based web servers and distributions commonly exposing misconfigured SQL interfaces and filesystem structures.</data>
  <data key="d9">applicable to,is relevant to technology stack,operates on,operates within,relevant to,targets</data>
